<p>Hey there again Oystershell, let's take a look at your edits!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So your idea's essentially the same, but you've explained it more specifically, which is good. I also like the idea of a complex game of seeing-but-not-seeing the future you've created (i.e predicting the future, but only really vaguely), along with the added complexity behind the Tower card seeing visions of the end of the world.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, this brings up a logistical problem: do the cards predict an absolute future (no matter what you do that future will come to pass) or a likely future? If it's the former, it has implications on timelines and free will (mostly <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatalism" target="_blank">Fatalism</a>/<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_determinism" target="_blank">Hard Determinism</a>), and if it's the latter then seeing the cards might alter the most likely possible future (due to interpretations of them). I think I might be overthinking this, but you may want to consider this.</p>
<p>One last thing: some of the more skeptical members of this Site don't like seeing Thaumiel SCPs created by the Foundation that the Foundation has a disproportionate amount of trust in (considering they're still anomalies), especially ones as borderline OP as this one. It can be viewed as cliche (especially when they fail epically, as this one did), and can serve to break the suspension of disbelief for some of the readers on the Site. To remedy this, I'd recommend contacting some reviewers from the <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/butterfly-squad-roster">Butterfly Squad</a> to get some alternative opinions on your concept, as my opinion is simply one of many.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Anyway, thanks for reading, and I hope this helps!<br />
<span style="font-size:x-small;">P.S For future reference, the one-sentence summary in my previous crit is there to help you get a view on the very basics that critters see when they read your concept, and thus help you get a more objective/wide look at your idea to see if you want to change anything.<br />
P.P.S <em>Damn</em> that was a long sentence.<br />
P.P.P.S Ok I'll stop bothering you now.</span></p>

							</div>

								
					
